\title{Markdown Style Guide}
\author{Ivan Jacob Agaloos Pesigan}
\date{}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

This guide covers \texttt{NEWS.md}, \texttt{README.Rmd},
vignettes (\texttt{.Rmd}, \texttt{.Rmd.orig}), Quarto (\texttt{.qmd}),
\texttt{AGENTS.md}, and the files in \texttt{.setup/ai} and \texttt{.setup/logs}.
It does not cover generated files
(\texttt{README.md}, vignettes built from \texttt{.setup/vignettes}).
Agents follow \texttt{.setup/ai/style/markdown.md},
which follows this guide and is changed together with it.

\section{Line Breaks}

Use semantic line breaks, as in \LaTeX{}.
There is no length limit.
Tables and link lines stay whole.
Existing files are reflowed when next edited;
there are no pushes for layout alone.

\section{Lists}

Use \texttt{-} for bullets everywhere, \texttt{NEWS.md} too.
Number lists \texttt{1.}, \texttt{2.}.

\section{Headings}

ATX headings (\texttt{\#}) only, one \texttt{\#} title per file, and no skipped levels.
\texttt{NEWS.md} keeps pkgdown's \texttt{\# <pkg> <version>} and \texttt{\#\# <section>}.

\section{Code}

Fenced code blocks name a language (\texttt{r}, \texttt{bash}, \texttt{latex}, \texttt{yaml}).
Use inline code for files, functions, commands, and variables.

\section{Emphasis and Links}

Use \texttt{**bold**} and \texttt{\_italic\_}.
Links are inline with descriptive text, not ``here''.
Bare URLs are in \texttt{<...>}.

\section{Math}

Math in \texttt{.Rmd} and \texttt{.qmd} files follows the math guide.

\section{Tools}

\texttt{make lint} reports (never fails on) bullets, headings, code fences, links, and math.
\texttt{lintr} already checks the R chunks.

\nocite{RCoreTeam-2026}

\printbibliography

\end{document}
